<header><p class="st-kicker">princely state · Indian princely states</p><h1 class="st-title">Charkhari State</h1><p class="st-meta">1765 CE – 1950 CE (approx.) · capital Charkhari</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Bundela Rajput state of Bundelkhand known for its lakes and hill fort, whose Raja stood by the British during the revolt of 1857.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Charkhari was founded around 1765 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khuman-singh-of-charkhari">Khuman Singh</a>, a descendant of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chhatrasal">Maharaja Chhatrasal</a> through the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/jaitpur-state">Jaitpur</a> branch, who received a share of the family lands. The British confirmed the state by sanad in 1804.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Charkhari was an 11-gun salute state of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bundelkhand-agency">Bundelkhand Agency</a> ruled by a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharaja">Maharaja</a> from the hill fort of Mangalgarh, ringed by artificial lakes. In 1857 Raja <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ratan-singh-of-charkhari">Ratan Singh</a> remained loyal to the British and was besieged by the rebel leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatya-tope">Tantia Tope</a>, for which he was later rewarded.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After independence Charkhari joined <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/vindhya-pradesh">Vindhya Pradesh</a> and was absorbed by 1950; the town is now in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahoba">Mahoba</a> district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uttar-pradesh">Uttar Pradesh</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/charkhari-state.jpg" alt="Charkhari State" width="200" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Original uploader was Nataraja at fr.wikipedia · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Drapeau_Charkhari.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1765 CE – 1950 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>princely state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Charkhari</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Khuman Singh, Ratan Singh</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Bundeli</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Charkhari</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1858&amp;focus=charkhari-state" aria-label="Charkhari State on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6792 1244 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Charkhari State lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7112 1500L7364 1601M7112 1500L7364 1602M7112 1500L7160 1530"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7364 1601h0M7364 1602h0M7160 1530h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7112 1500h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7112 1500h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Charkhari. <a href="/atlas?year=1858&amp;focus=charkhari-state">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
